WEBVTT


00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.990
What's a really good way to throw people off track?

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.930
Well, a great way is to not make it clear to them

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.330
where your sentences stop.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.530
These are technically known as run-on sentences,

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:14.935
though we like to think of them as runaway sentences.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.530
The term sounds like it means a sentence that

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:23.900
just goes on and on and on.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.560
And sometimes they do.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:29.910
But length isn't actually the issue. No.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.850
A run-on sentence is a common grammatical error

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:36.230
you make one by not using the right punctuation.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:37.937
Whoops, there's one now.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.960
Specifically, a run-on sentence is

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.140
where two or more main clauses are

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.680
joined together without the right punctuation

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.550
or joining word.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.450
You can tell we actually have two main clauses here,

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.580
because each part could stand alone as a separate sentence.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.470
So what's the big deal?

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:05.740
Well, like any grammatical mistake, they don't look good.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:09.930
And that means, if you write them, you don't look good.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.080
It's sad, but true.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.390
Your reader may just judge you harshly

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:19.520
if they think you can't keep your sentences under control.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.050
When we read, we build up meaning one section at a time.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.860
Each word affects how we understand

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.850
the ones that come after it.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:31.480
And punctuation marks also act as signals to tell you

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.950
where the sentence is going.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.190
Think of them like railway signals.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:39.176
They keep your reader on the right track.

00:01:39.176 --> 00:01:42.300
But when one main clause comes straight after another

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.540
without these signs, then your reader

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.350
could easily be derailed.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:53.090
When you need to fix a run-on sentence, a comma on its own

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:54.410
won't help.

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:57.000
A comma can do many, many things.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.310
But joining together two main clauses? Uh-uh.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.450
It's not strong enough.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.200
In fact, doing this creates a particular kind

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.479
of run-on sentence called a comma splice.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:11.770
But never fear, here are three easy ways

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.670
to fix any run-on sentence.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:18.110
The first is the good old full stop, or period.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:20.740
You simply put one after the first main clause,

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:24.990
and start the next main clause with a capital letter: done.

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.550
This approach works best when the sentences are not

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:29.650
closely related in meaning.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.920
But it can sound a bit choppy and disjointed,

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.334
especially if you use it too often.

00:02:36.334 --> 00:02:38.500
Maybe you want to show that there is some connection

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.080
between the two parts.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.570
In that case, you could go with option two: the semicolon.

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:48.650
A semicolon shows the two parts are linked in some way.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.750
But you might want to make it clear exactly how they

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:53.720
relate to each other.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:57.140
This brings us to option three: a comma

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.800
plus a coordinating conjunction.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.100
Whoa, hang on a second.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.780
So what the heck is a coordinating conjunction?

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:07.840
Well, although this could be an unfamiliar and scary-

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.600
sounding grammar term, the words it refers to

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.820
are more like old friends.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:15.370
They're a set of joining words.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:19.220
And you can remember them using the acronym FANBOYS.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:27.324
This stands for for, and, nor, but, or, yet, and so.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.450
The joining word you pick will make

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:37.080
the relationship between the two main clauses clear.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.360
So now you know how to spot and fix a run-on sentence,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.940
you can be sure of always keeping your writing

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:45.960
on the right track.

